    <h2>Chapter 3: Literary Devices, Figures of Speech &amp; Rhetoric</h2>

    <div class="content-content">
        <p>Literary devices and rhetorical figures are the tools writers and speakers use to convey meaning, create imagery, and persuade audiences. This chapter catalogs over 40 of the most important devices with definitions and classic examples.</p>

        <h3>3.1 Figurative Language</h3>
        <table class="ref-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Simile</strong></td><td>Comparison using <em>like</em> or <em>as</em></td><td><em>"My love is like a red, red rose." — Burns</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Metaphor</strong></td><td>Direct comparison without <em>like</em> or <em>as</em></td><td><em>"All the world's a stage." — Shakespeare</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Extended Metaphor</strong></td><td>Metaphor sustained throughout a passage or work</td><td>Life as a journey spanning an entire poem</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Personification</strong></td><td>Human attributes given to non-human things</td><td><em>"The wind howled its anger."</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Anthropomorphism</strong></td><td>Non-human characters behave as humans (narrative)</td><td>Talking animals in <em>Animal Farm</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Apostrophe</strong></td><td>Address to an absent person or abstract concept</td><td><em>"O Death, where is thy sting?" — Paul</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Hyperbole</strong></td><td>Deliberate exaggeration for effect</td><td><em>"I've told you a million times!"</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Understatement</strong></td><td>Deliberate minimization of something significant</td><td><em>"It's just a flesh wound." — Monty Python</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Litotes</strong></td><td>Understatement using double negation</td><td><em>"She's not unkind."</em> (meaning she's kind)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Irony (Verbal)</strong></td><td>Saying the opposite of what is meant</td><td><em>"Oh, great — another Monday!"</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Irony (Situational)</strong></td><td>Outcome opposite to what is expected</td><td>A fire station burning down</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Irony (Dramatic)</strong></td><td>Audience knows something characters don't</td><td>The audience knows Juliet is alive; Romeo doesn't</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Sarcasm</strong></td><td>Sharp, often bitter verbal irony intended to wound</td><td><em>"Oh, brilliant plan — let's all walk into the fire."</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Oxymoron</strong></td><td>Contradictory terms placed together</td><td><em>deafening silence, bittersweet, living death</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Paradox</strong></td><td>Statement that seems contradictory but reveals truth</td><td><em>"I must be cruel only to be kind." — Shakespeare</em></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>3.2 Sound Devices</h3>
        <table class="ref-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Alliteration</strong></td><td>Repetition of initial consonant sounds</td><td><em>"Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers."</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Assonance</strong></td><td>Repetition of vowel sounds within words</td><td><em>"The rain in Spain stays mainly on the plain."</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Consonance</strong></td><td>Repetition of consonant sounds within/at end of words</td><td><em>"pitter-patter," "struck a streak"</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Onomatopoeia</strong></td><td>Words that imitate natural sounds</td><td><em>buzz, crash, hiss, murmur, sizzle</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Cacophony</strong></td><td>Harsh, discordant sounds creating tension</td><td><em>"Beware the Jabberwock, my son! The jaws that bite, the claws that catch!"</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Euphony</strong></td><td>Pleasing, harmonious sounds creating calm</td><td><em>"Soft is the music that would charm forever…"</em></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>3.3 Structural &amp; Rhetorical Devices</h3>
        <table class="ref-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Anaphora</strong></td><td>Repetition of a word/phrase at the start of successive clauses</td><td><em>"We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds…" — Churchill</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Epistrophe</strong></td><td>Repetition at the end of successive clauses</td><td><em>"…of the people, by the people, for the people." — Lincoln</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Symploce</strong></td><td>Combination of anaphora and epistrophe</td><td>Repeated beginning and ending words across clauses</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Chiasmus</strong></td><td>Reversed grammatical structure in successive clauses (ABBA)</td><td><em>"Ask not what your country can do for you — ask what you can do for your country." — JFK</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Antithesis</strong></td><td>Juxtaposition of contrasting ideas in parallel structure</td><td><em>"It was the best of times, it was the worst of times." — Dickens</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Parallelism</strong></td><td>Similar grammatical structure repeated for emphasis</td><td><em>"I came, I saw, I conquered."</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Asyndeton</strong></td><td>Omission of conjunctions for rapid, forceful effect</td><td><em>"I came, I saw, I conquered."</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Polysyndeton</strong></td><td>Excessive use of conjunctions to slow pace</td><td><em>"We laughed and danced and sang and ate all night."</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Anadiplosis</strong></td><td>End of one clause repeated at the start of the next</td><td><em>"Fear leads to anger. Anger leads to hate. Hate leads to suffering." — Yoda</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Zeugma</strong></td><td>One verb governs multiple objects with different meanings</td><td><em>"She lost her keys and her temper."</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Ellipsis</strong></td><td>Omission of words understood from context</td><td><em>"To err is human; to forgive [is] divine."</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Rhetorical Question</strong></td><td>Question posed for effect, not expecting an answer</td><td><em>"Is this the face that launched a thousand ships?" — Marlowe</em></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>3.4 Narrative &amp; Descriptive Devices</h3>
        <table class="ref-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Allusion</strong></td><td>Indirect reference to a historical, cultural, or literary event</td><td><em>"He's a real Romeo with the ladies."</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Symbol</strong></td><td>Object representing an abstract idea beyond its literal meaning</td><td>The green light in <em>The Great Gatsby</em> = hope/the American Dream</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Allegory</strong></td><td>Narrative where characters/events symbolize larger truths</td><td><em>Animal Farm</em> as an allegory for Stalinist Russia</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Foreshadowing</strong></td><td>Hints or clues about future events</td><td>The witches' prophecy in <em>Macbeth</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Flashback</strong></td><td>Interruption of chronological narrative to show past events</td><td>Pip's childhood memories in <em>Great Expectations</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>In medias res</strong></td><td>Story begins in the middle of the action</td><td><em>The Iliad</em> opens during the Trojan War, not its beginning</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Imagery</strong></td><td>Descriptive language appealing to the five senses</td><td><em>"The fog comes on little cat feet." — Sandburg</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Synesthesia</strong></td><td>Description of one sense in terms of another</td><td><em>"a loud perfume," "a sharp color"</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Motif</strong></td><td>Recurring element carrying symbolic meaning</td><td>The mockingbird in <em>To Kill a Mockingbird</em></td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Archetype</strong></td><td>Universal character type, symbol, or plot pattern</td><td>The hero's journey, the trickster, the mentor</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Deus ex machina</strong></td><td>Improbable resolution introduced suddenly to solve a plot</td><td>A character unexpectedly rescued at the last moment</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Synecdoche</strong></td><td>Part used to represent the whole (or vice versa)</td><td><em>"All hands on deck"</em> (hands = sailors)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Metonymy</strong></td><td>Substituting an associated word for the thing itself</td><td><em>"The pen is mightier than the sword"</em> (pen = writing, sword = military force)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Euphemism</strong></td><td>Mild expression substituted for a harsh one</td><td><em>"passed away"</em> for died; <em>"let go"</em> for fired</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>3.5 The Three Aristotelian Appeals (Rhetoric)</h3>
        <table class="ref-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Appeal</th><th>Definition</th><th>Strategy</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Ethos</strong></td><td>Appeal to the speaker's credibility and character</td><td>Cite credentials, demonstrate fairness and expertise</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Pathos</strong></td><td>Appeal to the audience's emotions</td><td>Use vivid imagery, personal anecdotes, emotionally resonant language</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Logos</strong></td><td>Appeal to logic and reason</td><td>Provide evidence, statistics, logical arguments, and syllogisms</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p><strong>Kairos</strong>: The opportune moment — timing an argument for maximum persuasive effect.</p>
        <p><strong>Telos</strong>: The purpose or ultimate aim of the rhetorical act.</p>
    </div>
</div>
